     "text": [
      "LIOUVILLE.  For a completely generic H(q,p):\n   X_H =\n         ['diff(HH,p1,1),'diff(HH,p2,1),'diff(HH,p3,1),-'diff(HH,q1,1),\n          -'diff(HH,q2,1),-'diff(HH,q3,1)]\n   div X_H = Σ ∂(X_H)^k/∂z^k =  0\n   The mixed partials cancel in pairs: ∂²H/∂q∂p from the q-block\n   against −∂²H/∂p∂q from the p-block.  Phase-space volume is\n   conserved by EVERY Hamiltonian flow, whatever H is.\nSYMPLECTIC, NOT ISOMETRIC.  Jacobians of the time-t flow map:\n    harmonic oscillator, H = (p² + q²)/2\n      M =  matrix([cos(tv),sin(tv)],[-sin(tv),cos(tv)])\n      MᵀΩM − Ω =  matrix([0,0],[0,0])      det M =  1\n    free particle, H = p²/2\n      M =  matrix([1,tv],[0,1])\n      MᵀΩM − Ω =  matrix([0,0],[0,0])      det M =  1\nBoth preserve ω and both have determinant 1, so both preserve AREA.\nBut the free-particle map is a shear: a round blob of initial\nconditions becomes a long thin sliver, of the same area.  No metric\nsurvives the flow; only the area does.  Phase space has no notion\nof length or angle to lose.\nCONTRAST: the damped oscillator q̇ = p, ṗ = −q − 2γp.\n   div X =  -(2*gam)    ≠ 0 for γ > 0\n   Volume shrinks at the rate e^{−2γt}: trajectories collapse onto\n   an attractor.  That cannot happen in a Hamiltonian system, and\n   the obstruction is exactly div X_H = 0 above.  Damping is not a\n   force one can put in H; it is a statement that the system is not\n   closed."
