     "text": [
      "angular momentum L = q × p, as three functions on phase space:\n   {Lᵢ,Lⱼ} − ε_ijk Lₖ :\n      i= 1  j= 2  :  0\n      i= 1  j= 3  :  0\n      i= 2  j= 3  :  0\n   The SAME structure constants as so(3) in 5.01 — where they were\n   matrices, and in 1.01, where they were the cross product.  Here\n   they are functions, and the bracket is Poisson's.  One algebra,\n   three incarnations.\nfor ANY central Hamiltonian H = |p|²/2 + V(|q|) :\n   {L 1 , H} =  0\n   {L 2 , H} =  0\n   {L 3 , H} =  0\n   Rotational symmetry ⟹ angular momentum conserved.  That is\n   Noether's theorem, and geometrically it is one line: the flow of\n   Lᵢ preserves H, so H's flow preserves Lᵢ.  Antisymmetry of the\n   bracket does the work.\nKEPLER, H = |p|²/2 − k/|q|.  The Laplace–Runge–Lenz vector\n   A = p × L − k q/|q| :\n   A₁ =  -((k*q1)/sqrt(q3^2+q2^2+q1^2))-p3*(p1*q3-p3*q1)+p2*(p2*q1-p1*q2)\n   {Aᵢ, H} :\n      i =  1  :  0\n      i =  2  :  0\n      i =  3  :  0\n   Conserved — and NOT a consequence of rotational symmetry, since\n   it is a vector independent of L.  Kepler's problem has a hidden\n   symmetry that a general central force does not.\n   {Lᵢ, Aⱼ} − ε_ijk Aₖ :\n      i= 1  j= 2  :  0\n      i= 1  j= 3  :  0\n      i= 2  j= 3  :  0\n      (so A transforms as a vector under rotations, as it must)\n   {Aᵢ, Aⱼ} + 2H ε_ijk Lₖ :\n      i= 1  j= 2  :  0\n      i= 1  j= 3  :  0\n      i= 2  j= 3  :  0\nOn a bound energy shell H = E < 0, rescaling A by 1/√(−2E) turns\nthat last line into {Ãᵢ,Ãⱼ} = ε_ijk Lₖ.  Together with {L,L} and\n{L,Ã} the six functions close into so(4) — a bigger symmetry than\nthe rotations one can see.\nTHAT is why Kepler orbits close.  The extra conserved vector points\nalong the major axis and pins it in place, so the ellipse cannot\nprecess.  6.02 added one term, −2ML²/r³, to the effective potential;\nit breaks the hidden symmetry, A is no longer conserved, and the\naxis drifts by 6πM/(a(1−e²)) per orbit.  Mercury's 43″ per century\nis the measurement of a broken symmetry."
