     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "DARBOUX, explicitly, in two dimensions.\n   ω =  %e^xx  dx ∧ dy\n   try X =  %e^xx ,  Y =  yy\n   Jacobian =  matrix([%e^xx,0],[0,1])    det =  %e^xx\n   dX ∧ dY = (det J) dx ∧ dy =  %e^xx  dx ∧ dy\n   difference from ω :  0\n   So in the coordinates (X,Y) the form is exactly dX ∧ dY.\n   Any positive density can be flattened the same way — pick X with\n   dX/dx equal to the density and leave Y alone.  In 2n dimensions\n   the same conclusion holds, by Moser's argument.\nCONTRAST.  Try the same trick on a METRIC  g = dx² + f(x)² dy².\n   K = −f″/f  (3.04, 5.05), a function of the metric alone, so no\n   change of coordinates can alter it.  Unless f″ = 0 there are\n   therefore NO coordinates making g the identity on a neighbourhood.\n      f = sin x  :  K =  1    the unit sphere\n      f = sinh x :  K =  -1    the hyperbolic plane\n      f = x      :  K =  0    the flat plane in polar coordinates (4.04)\n   Only the last can be flattened, and 4.04 showed exactly that:\n   Γ ≠ 0 there, but R ≡ 0.\n   A metric can be made δᵢⱼ AT A POINT with vanishing first\n   derivatives (4.03's normal coordinates), and no further.  The\n   second derivatives survive as curvature, and Stage 4 is the study\n   of that obstruction.\n   A symplectic form has no obstruction at any order.  Two symplectic\n   manifolds of the same dimension are locally indistinguishable —\n   there is no symplectic curvature to distinguish them.  Everything\n   interesting is therefore GLOBAL: symplectic capacities, Gromov's\n   non-squeezing theorem, Floer homology.  It is a topological\n   subject wearing geometric clothes."
     ]
