   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## Summary\n\n$$\\{f,g\\} = \\sum_i \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial q^i}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial p_i} - \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial p_i}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial q^i}, \\qquad \\dot f = \\{f, H\\}$$\n\n**Antisymmetric instead of symmetric, and everything changes.** $\\det\\Omega = 1$, so $\\omega$ is non-degenerate; in odd dimension an antisymmetric matrix is always singular, which is why symplectic manifolds are even-dimensional. That is 5.06's Pfaffian argument again.\n\n**Functions form a Lie algebra.** Antisymmetry, Jacobi and Leibniz were all verified identically zero on three generic functions of six variables. The axioms are 5.01's, with $\\{\\cdot,\\cdot\\}$ for $[\\cdot,\\cdot]$; Leibniz is the extra ingredient that makes it a *Poisson* algebra rather than merely a Lie one.\n\nSolving $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega = dH$ as the linear system $\\Omega X = \\nabla H$ returned $X_H = (\\partial H/\\partial p, -\\partial H/\\partial q)$ — Hamilton's equations, read straight off. And $\\{q_1,H\\} = \\partial H/\\partial p_1$, $\\{p_1,H\\} = -\\partial H/\\partial q_1$: *conserved* and *Poisson-commutes with $H$* are one statement.\n\n**Liouville is a cancellation of mixed partials.** For a completely generic $H$, $\\operatorname{div}X_H = 0$ exactly — the $\\partial^2H/\\partial q\\partial p$ terms from the $q$-block cancel the $-\\partial^2H/\\partial p\\partial q$ terms from the $p$-block. Phase-space volume is conserved by *every* Hamiltonian flow, whatever $H$ is.\n\nThe damped oscillator gives $\\operatorname{div}X = -2\\gamma \\ne 0$: trajectories collapse onto an attractor. That cannot happen in a Hamiltonian system, and the obstruction is precisely the identity above. **Damping is not a force one can put in $H$**; it is a statement that the system is not closed.\n\n**Symplectic, not isometric.** Both the harmonic-oscillator flow $\\begin{psmallmatrix}\\cos t&\\sin t\\\\-\\sin t&\\cos t\\end{psmallmatrix}$ and the free-particle shear $\\begin{psmallmatrix}1&t\\\\0&1\\end{psmallmatrix}$ satisfy $M^T\\Omega M = \\Omega$ with $\\det M = 1$. Both preserve area; the second turns a round blob into a long thin sliver of the same area. There is no length or angle in phase space to preserve.\n\n**$\\mathfrak{so}(3)$, a third time.** $\\{L_i,L_j\\} = \\varepsilon_{ijk}L_k$ exactly, with the same structure constants as the matrices of 5.01 and the cross product of 1.01. One algebra, three incarnations. And $\\{L_i, H\\} = 0$ for *any* central $H = |p|^2/2 + V(|q|)$ — Noether's theorem in one line, powered by the antisymmetry of the bracket.\n\n**Kepler's hidden symmetry, and why Mercury precesses.** For $H = |p|^2/2 - k/|q|$ the Laplace–Runge–Lenz vector $A = p\\times L - k\\hat q$ satisfies, all verified identically:\n\n$$\\{A_i, H\\} = 0, \\qquad \\{L_i,A_j\\} = \\varepsilon_{ijk}A_k, \\qquad \\{A_i,A_j\\} = -2H\\,\\varepsilon_{ijk}L_k.$$\n\nOn a bound shell $H = E < 0$, rescaling $A$ by $1/\\sqrt{-2E}$ closes the six functions into $\\mathfrak{so}(4)$ — a symmetry strictly larger than the visible rotations. $A$ points along the major axis and pins it, which is *why* Kepler ellipses close.\n\n6.02 added exactly one term to the effective potential, $-2ML^2/r^3$. It breaks this hidden symmetry, $A$ stops being conserved, and the axis drifts by $6\\pi M/(a(1-e^2))$ per orbit. **Mercury's 43 arcseconds per century is the measurement of a broken symmetry** — and this notebook and the last one now meet from opposite directions.\n\n**Darboux: no curvature at all.** $\\omega = e^x\\,dx\\wedge dy$ becomes exactly $dX\\wedge dY$ under $X = e^x$, $Y = y$ — the Jacobian determinant matches the density with difference zero. Any positive density flattens the same way.\n\nA metric does not. For $g = dx^2 + f(x)^2dy^2$, $K = -f''/f$ came out $1$, $-1$, $0$ for $f = \\sin x$, $\\sinh x$, $x$; $K$ depends on the metric alone, so no change of coordinates can touch it. Only the flat case can be flattened — which is 4.04's polar-coordinate plane, $\\Gamma \\ne 0$ with $R \\equiv 0$.\n\n**So symplectic geometry has no local invariants at any order.** Everything interesting is global from the first line: capacities, non-squeezing, Floer homology. It is a topological subject in geometric clothes.\n\n## Next\n\n**6.04 — Complex and Kähler Geometry** puts three structures on one manifold — a metric, a symplectic form and a complex structure — and requires them to be compatible. Any two determine the third. Kähler manifolds are where Riemannian geometry, symplectic geometry and complex analysis are the same subject.\n\n## References\n\n- ★ Lee, T., Leok, M. and McClamroch, N.H. (2018) *Global Formulations of Lagrangian and Hamiltonian Dynamics on Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. Mechanics done geometrically from the start, and the closest thing here to a computational treatment.\n- Arnold, V.I. (1989) *Mathematical Methods of Classical Mechanics*. 2nd edn. Graduate Texts in Mathematics 60. New York: Springer. Chapters 8–9. Still the book: Darboux, Liouville, the moment map and the Kepler symmetry, all in Arnold's geometric idiom.\n- McDuff, D. and Salamon, D. (2017) *Introduction to Symplectic Topology*. 3rd edn. Oxford: Oxford University Press. Where \"no local invariants, so study the global ones\" becomes a research programme — non-squeezing, capacities, Floer theory.\n- ★ Landau, L.D. and Lifshitz, E.M. (1976) *Mechanics*. 3rd edn. Course of Theoretical Physics vol. 1. Oxford: Pergamon. §§40–46 for Poisson brackets and canonical transformations, with no manifolds mentioned and nothing lost.\n- Marsden, J.E. and Ratiu, T.S. (1999) *Introduction to Mechanics and Symmetry*. 2nd edn. New York: Springer. The moment map and symplectic reduction in full — the machinery this notebook only gestures at.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell was executed. Two Maxima notes: `gradef(Vgen(x), Vp(x))` is required before the generic central-potential brackets, or the chain rule is not applied and the brackets print as non-zero expressions that merely happen to cancel; and curvature comparisons are computed from an explicit $f$ rather than by substituting into a `depends`-based noun derivative, which does not evaluate.*",
   "metadata": {}
  }
 ]
